%I A090057 #7 Jul 29 2026 09:47:17
%S A090057 1050,1080,3105,5100,5400,7020,7030,9207,9801,10010,10050,10080,10098,
%T A090057 10200,10206,20020,20160,20250,20304,20400,20500,20790,21000,21060,
%U A090057 30015,30030,30105,30240,30420,30450,30600,35100,40040,40050
%N A090057 Numbers k divisible by exactly two nontrivial permutations (rearrangements) of the digits of k.
%C A090057 Trivial permutations are identified as (1) permutation = k, or (2) when k mod 10=0, permutations of k's digits which result in shifting only trailing zeros to the most significant side of k where they drop off, such that permutation = k/10^z, where z <= the number of trailing zeros of k. So if k were 1809000, the following permutations would be excluded as trivial: 1809000, 0180900, 0018090, 0001809.
%H A090057 Chuck Seggelin, <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20040109232550/http://www.plastereddragon.com/maths/asortdiv.htm">Numbers Divisible by Digit Permutations</a>.
%e A090057 3105 is a term because 3105 is divisible by both 135 and 1035, two nontrivial permutations of 3105.
%e A090057 9207 is a term because 9207 is divisible by both 279 and 297, two nontrivial permutations of 9207.
%Y A090057 Cf. A090055, A090058, A090059, A090060, A090061.
%K A090057 nonn,base
%O A090057 1,1
%A A090057 _Chuck Seggelin_, Nov 21 2003
